Surface: free a surface without joining its threads on the main thread

ghostty_surface_free joins a surface's renderer and IO threads on the
calling thread, which for an embedder is the main thread. Those threads
push to the app mailbox with no timeout when it is full, only the main
thread drains it (ghostty_app_tick), and the IO thread joins its reader
on the way out. So a main thread that let the mailbox fill and then
freed a surface waits on itself for good. Muster's window did, for three
hours, while the machine was swapping and seven agents were setting
titles and progress faster than the stalled main thread could drain.

This adds ghostty_surface_free_detached, which splits the free in three:

- On the calling thread, which must be the main thread: the half of
  apprt.embedded.Surface.deinit that touches the app. The inspector and
  the title are freed and the surface leaves the app's list, so App's
  hasSurface drops whatever is still queued for it. The Metal layer's
  display callback is cleared too, because the display pass calls into
  the renderer on the main thread while its thread is stopping.
- On a spawned, detached thread: Surface.stopThreads, which stops and
  joins the search, renderer and IO threads. This is the part that can
  wait on the mailbox.
- Back on the main queue, once they have exited: Surface.deinitStopped,
  the surface's memory, then done(userdata). The renderer's teardown
  stops its CVDisplayLink, which upstream requires on the main thread
  (#14691): stopping it elsewhere collides with CoreVideo during display
  sleep, wake or reconfiguration and can freeze the app, and the link's
  main-queue sync callback can race its own destruction. The font grid
  reference, which the app shares, is also released there.

If the thread cannot be spawned, every part runs on the caller, which is
what ghostty_surface_free does.

The caller must not free the app before done has been called, and must
keep servicing the main queue until then.

Not purely additive: Surface.deinit is split into stopThreads and
deinitStopped, which it calls in order, so its behavior is unchanged but
a re-pin can conflict there. Otherwise one new function; ghostty_surface_free
is unchanged. Muster carries this for good rather than sending it upstream.

